var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"yeast/#Yeast","page":"Yeast","text":"The number of yeast cells to pitch.","title":"Yeast"},{"category":"function","location":"yeast/#BrewingFormulas.pitch_cells","page":"Yeast","text":"pitch_cells(volume, gravity; rate = 0.75, eq = SugarGravityQuad())\n\nThe number of yeast cells to pitch in a volume of wort of the given gravity, at rate million cells per mL per °P (usually 0.75 for ales and 1.5 for lagers).\n\nReferences\n\nThe rates 0.75 (ales) and 1.5 (lagers) million cells/mL/°P are commonly attributed to G. Fix, as in C. White and J. Zainasheff, Yeast: The Practical Guide to Beer Fermentation, Brewers Publications, 2010.\n\njulia> pitch_cells(20u\"L\", 12u\"°P\")\n1.8e11\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.pitch_cells"},{"category":"section","location":"refractometer/#Refractometer","page":"Refractometer","text":"Refractometers are calibrated for sucrose solutions; wort reads higher by the wort correction factor, and once fermentation starts the alcohol distorts the reading, so the final gravity is estimated by an empirical fit.","title":"Refractometer"},{"category":"function","location":"refractometer/#BrewingFormulas.refractometer_gravity","page":"Refractometer","text":"refractometer_gravity(reading; wcf = 1.04)\n\nThe gravity, in °P, of an unfermented wort from the refractometer reading (in Brix), corrected by the wort correction factor wcf. The default 1.04 is the value commonly used (and the factor for wort in the Python BrewQuiver); it is best determined for each refractometer against a hydrometer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.refractometer_gravity"},{"category":"function","location":"refractometer/#BrewingFormulas.refractometer_final_gravity","page":"Refractometer","text":"refractometer_final_gravity([model = TerrillCubic()], initial, final; wcf = 1.04)\n\nThe apparent final gravity, in sg, estimated from the refractometer readings (in Brix) of the wort before fermentation, initial, and of the fermented beer, final, both corrected by the wort correction factor wcf, with Sean Terrill's fits (TerrillCubic or TerrillLinear).\n\nReferences\n\nS. Terrill, \"Refractometer FG Results\", 7 April 2011, http://seanterrill.com/2011/04/07/refractometer-fg-results/ (cited by the Python BrewQuiver).\n\njulia> refractometer_final_gravity(12u\"Brix\", 6.5u\"Brix\")\n1.0129859456476515 sg\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.refractometer_final_gravity"},{"category":"section","location":"bitterness/#Bitterness","page":"Bitterness","text":"The utilization of alpha acids in the boil, given by one of the bitterness models, and the resulting bitterness, taking 1 IBU as 1 mg/L of iso-alpha acids. The isomerization below boiling (hop stands, whirlpools) is given by the rate of isomerization relative to boiling.","title":"Bitterness"},{"category":"function","location":"bitterness/#BrewingFormulas.utilization","page":"Bitterness","text":"utilization([model = Tinseth()], gravity, time; eq = SugarGravityQuad())\n\nThe fraction of alpha acids isomerized in a wort of the given (boil) gravity after boiling for time, with the given BitternessModel; see the models for the references.\n\njulia> utilization(1.050u\"sg\", 60u\"minute\")\n0.2306640772510991\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.utilization"},{"category":"function","location":"bitterness/#BrewingFormulas.isomerization_rate","page":"Bitterness","text":"isomerization_rate(temperature)\n\nThe first-order rate constant, per minute, of the isomerization of alpha acids into iso-alpha acids in wort at temperature, by the Arrhenius fit of Malowicki and Shellhammer: k = 7.9 × 10¹¹ exp(-11858 K / T) per minute, about 0.0125 per minute at 100 °C. See isomerization_factor.\n\nReferences\n\nM. G. Malowicki and T. H. Shellhammer, \"Isomerization and Degradation Kinetics of Hop (Humulus lupulus) Acids in a Model Wort-Boiling System\", J. Agric. Food Chem. 53 (2005) 4434–4439, https://doi.org/10.1021/jf0481296. The constants are those quoted from this paper in brewing calculators; they were not checked against the paper itself.\n\njulia> ustrip(u\"minute^-1\", isomerization_rate(100u\"°C\"))   # per minute, at 100 °C\n0.01249025501937107\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.isomerization_rate"},{"category":"function","location":"bitterness/#BrewingFormulas.isomerization_factor","page":"Bitterness","text":"isomerization_factor(temperature; reference = 100u\"°C\")\n\nThe rate of isomerization of alpha acids at temperature relative to the rate at reference (boiling), by the Arrhenius fit of isomerization_rate: about 0.71 at 96 °C, 0.42 at 90 °C and 0.20 at 82 °C, and negligible below 60 °C. A hop stand or whirlpool of time t at temperature then isomerizes about as much as a boil of isomerization_factor(temperature) * t.\n\njulia> isomerization_factor(90u\"°C\")\n0.41683354891657887\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.isomerization_factor"},{"category":"function","location":"bitterness/#BrewingFormulas.alpha_acid_mass","page":"Bitterness","text":"alpha_acid_mass(mass, alpha)\n\nThe mass of alpha acids, in g, in a mass of hops with the alpha acid content alpha (a percentage or fraction).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.alpha_acid_mass"},{"category":"function","location":"bitterness/#BrewingFormulas.isomerized_alpha_acids","page":"Bitterness","text":"isomerized_alpha_acids([model = Tinseth()], mass, alpha, gravity, time; factor = 1, eq = SugarGravityQuad())\n\nThe mass, in mg, of iso-alpha acids from a mass of hops with alpha acid content alpha, boiled for time in a wort of the given gravity, with the utilization given by the BitternessModel times factor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.isomerized_alpha_acids"},{"category":"function","location":"bitterness/#BrewingFormulas.ibu","page":"Bitterness","text":"ibu(isoalpha, volume)\nibu([model = Tinseth()], mass, alpha, gravity, time, volume; factor = 1, eq = SugarGravityQuad())\n\nThe bitterness, in IBU, of a volume of wort with the mass isoalpha of iso-alpha acids, 1 IBU being taken as 1 mg/L, the usual approximation. In the second form, the iso-alpha acids come from a mass of hops with alpha acid content alpha boiled for time in a wort of the given gravity, as in isomerized_alpha_acids.\n\njulia> ibu(30u\"g\", 10u\"percent\", 1.050u\"sg\", 60u\"minute\", 20u\"L\")\n34.59961158766487 IBU\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.ibu"},{"category":"section","location":"color/#Color","page":"Color","text":"Malt color units (MCU), the color of the grains times their mass over the volume of wort, are additive; they are converted to the SRM color of the wort by one of the color models.","title":"Color"},{"category":"function","location":"color/#BrewingFormulas.mcu","page":"Color","text":"mcu(mass, color, volume)\nmcu(bill, volume)\n\nThe malt color units, MCU = mass(lb) × color(°L) / volume(gal), of a mass of grain of the given color (in °L, SRM or EBC) in a volume of wort. With a bill, an iterable of mass => color pairs, the sum of the MCUs of the grains. MCUs are additive, unlike SRM.\n\nReferences\n\nD. Morey, \"Approximating SRM Beer Color of Homebrew Based on Recipe Formulation\", 2015, https://bwelman.github.io/bier/beer-color/beer-color.html.\n\njulia> mcu(4u\"kg\", 3u\"°L\", 20u\"L\")\n5.007242671211599\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.mcu"},{"category":"function","location":"color/#BrewingFormulas.srm","page":"Color","text":"srm([model = Morey()], mcu)\n\nThe color, in SRM, of a wort with the given malt color units mcu, with the given ColorModel.\n\njulia> srm(10)\n7.239807780133753 SRM\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.srm"},{"category":"function","location":"color/#BrewingFormulas.wort_color","page":"Color","text":"wort_color([model = Morey()], bill, volume)\n\nThe color, in SRM, of a volume of wort with the grains of bill, an iterable of mass => color pairs: srm(model, mcu(bill, volume)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.wort_color"},{"category":"function","location":"color/#BrewingFormulas.srm_rgb","page":"Color","text":"srm_rgb(color)\n\nAn (r, g, b) tuple of integers in 0:255 representing a beer of the given color (in SRM, EBC or °L), interpolated in a table from 1 to 40 SRM; colors outside this range are clamped to it.\n\nReferences\n\nThe table comes from the Python BrewQuiver, which gives no source for it, and no source was found. For a physically based alternative, see A. J. deLange's spectral model of beer color, implemented e.g. in https://pypi.org/project/olfarve/.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.srm_rgb"},{"category":"function","location":"color/#BrewingFormulas.srm_hex","page":"Color","text":"srm_hex(color)\n\nThe hexadecimal RGB string, as \"#rrggbb\", of srm_rgb(color).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.srm_hex"},{"category":"section","location":"boil/#Boil-and-volumes","page":"Boil and volumes","text":"The density of water as a function of temperature, used for the thermal contraction of wort, and the evaporation in the boil.","title":"Boil and volumes"},{"category":"function","location":"boil/#BrewingFormulas.water_density","page":"Boil and volumes","text":"water_density(temperature)\n\nThe density of water, in kg/L, at the given temperature, by Kell's formula (1975), valid from 0 to 150 °C.\n\nReferences\n\nG. S. Kell, \"Density, thermal expansivity, and compressibility of liquid water from 0° to 150 °C\", J. Chem. Eng. Data 20 (1975) 97–105, https://doi.org/10.1021/je60064a005.\n\njulia> water_density(20u\"°C\")\n0.9982041322005837 kg L⁻¹\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.water_density"},{"category":"function","location":"boil/#BrewingFormulas.volume_at_temperature","page":"Boil and volumes","text":"volume_at_temperature(volume, from, to)\n\nThe volume, at temperature to, of a volume of liquid measured at temperature from, taking its thermal expansion as that of water. E.g. the shrinkage of a wort when cooled.\n\njulia> volume_at_temperature(25u\"L\", 100u\"°C\", 20u\"°C\")\n24.002196197557904 L\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.volume_at_temperature"},{"category":"function","location":"boil/#BrewingFormulas.evaporated_volume","page":"Boil and volumes","text":"evaporated_volume(volume, rate, time)\n\nThe volume evaporated from a volume of boiling wort during time, with the evaporation rate either as a volume per time (e.g. 4u\"L/hr\") or as a fraction of volume per time (e.g. 10u\"percent/hr\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.evaporated_volume"},{"category":"section","location":"gravity/#Gravity-and-extract","page":"Gravity and extract","text":"Degrees Plato are the mass percentage of extract in a solution, and the specific gravity is its density relative to water, both at 20 °C. These definitions relate the gravity, the volume and the mass of extract, and give the volume of a wort from its water and dissolved extract.","title":"Gravity and extract"},{"category":"function","location":"gravity/#BrewingFormulas.plato","page":"Gravity and extract","text":"plato(gravity; eq = SugarGravityQuad())\n\nThe gravity (in °P, Brix, sg or gu) in degrees Plato.\n\njulia> plato(1.050u\"sg\")\n12.387625000000002 °P\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.plato"},{"category":"function","location":"gravity/#BrewingFormulas.specific_gravity","page":"Gravity and extract","text":"specific_gravity(gravity; eq = SugarGravityQuad())\n\nThe gravity (in °P, Brix, sg or gu) as specific gravity, in sg.\n\njulia> specific_gravity(12u\"°P\")\n1.0483700887844734 sg\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.specific_gravity"},{"category":"function","location":"gravity/#BrewingFormulas.extract_mass","page":"Gravity and extract","text":"extract_mass(volume, gravity; eq = SugarGravityQuad())\n\nThe mass of extract dissolved in a volume of solution with the given gravity, both at 20 °C: volume × sg × ρw × P / 100, where ρw is the density of water at 20 °C. This follows from the definitions of degrees Plato (percentage of extract by mass) and of specific gravity (density relative to water, both at 20 °C).\n\njulia> extract_mass(20u\"L\", 12u\"°P\")\n2.5115668025638263 kg\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.extract_mass"},{"category":"function","location":"gravity/#BrewingFormulas.gravity_from_extract","page":"Gravity and extract","text":"gravity_from_extract(extract, volume; eq = SugarGravityQuad())\n\nThe gravity, in °P, of a volume of solution containing the mass extract of dissolved extract; the inverse of extract_mass.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.gravity_from_extract"},{"category":"function","location":"gravity/#BrewingFormulas.volume_for_gravity","page":"Gravity and extract","text":"volume_for_gravity(volume, gravity, target; eq = SugarGravityQuad())\n\nThe volume a solution with the given volume and gravity must have, after dilution or concentration, to reach the target gravity, keeping its extract. Add water if it is larger than volume, boil off otherwise.\n\njulia> volume_for_gravity(20u\"L\", 1.060u\"sg\", 1.050u\"sg\")\n24.027752581421268 L\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.volume_for_gravity"},{"category":"function","location":"gravity/#BrewingFormulas.solution_gravity","page":"Gravity and extract","text":"solution_gravity(water, extract; eq = SugarGravityQuad())\n\nThe gravity, in °P, of the solution of the mass extract in water, given either as a mass or as a volume at 20 °C: P = 100 mₑ / (m_w + mₑ).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.solution_gravity"},{"category":"function","location":"gravity/#BrewingFormulas.solution_volume","page":"Gravity and extract","text":"solution_volume(water, extract; eq = SugarGravityQuad())\n\nThe volume, at 20 °C, of the solution of the mass extract in water, given either as a mass or as a volume at 20 °C: V = (m_w + mₑ) / (sg ρw), with sg the specific gravity of the solution. This is how the volume of a wort follows from the water and the extract dissolved from the grains.\n\njulia> solution_volume(20u\"L\", 2.5u\"kg\")\n21.54110091391626 L\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.solution_volume"},{"category":"function","location":"gravity/#BrewingFormulas.hydrometer_correction","page":"Gravity and extract","text":"hydrometer_correction(reading, temperature; calibration = 20u\"°C\", eq = SugarGravityQuad())\n\nThe gravity corrected from a hydrometer reading taken at temperature, for a hydrometer calibrated at calibration, in the unit of reading.\n\nThe correction is the difference, in thousandths of sg, of the cubic fit 1.313454 - 0.132674 T + 2.057793e-3 T² - 2.627634e-6 T³ (T in °F) at the two temperatures. The fit (R² = 0.999969, from 0 to 212 °F) is to the density of water from the CRC Handbook of Chemistry and Physics, relative to its value at 59 °F; taking differences allows any calibration temperature.\n\nReferences\n\nC. Lyons, \"Hydrometer Temperature Corrections\", Homebrew Digest #963, 7 September 1992, https://brewery.org/library/HydromCorr0992.html.\n\njulia> hydrometer_correction(1.046u\"sg\", 30u\"°C\")\n1.0484709600383841 sg\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.hydrometer_correction"},{"category":"function","location":"gravity/#BrewingFormulas.potential_from_yield","page":"Gravity and extract","text":"potential_from_yield(yield)\n\nThe potential of a fermentable, as the specific gravity of 1 lb in 1 US gallon (as in BeerJSON), from its extract yield relative to sucrose (a percentage or fraction).\n\njulia> potential_from_yield(80u\"percent\")\n1.0369712 sg\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.potential_from_yield"},{"category":"function","location":"gravity/#BrewingFormulas.yield_from_potential","page":"Gravity and extract","text":"yield_from_potential(potential)\n\nThe extract yield, relative to sucrose, of a fermentable with the given potential (the specific gravity of 1 lb in 1 US gallon); the inverse of potential_from_yield.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.yield_from_potential"},{"category":"function","location":"gravity/#BrewingFormulas.potential_extract","page":"Gravity and extract","text":"potential_extract(mass, yield; moisture = 0)\n\nThe maximum mass of extract obtainable from a mass of fermentable with the given extract yield on dry basis and moisture content (percentages or fractions).\n\njulia> potential_extract(5u\"kg\", 80u\"percent\"; moisture = 4u\"percent\")\n3.84 kg\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.potential_extract"},{"category":"constant","location":"gravity/#BrewingFormulas.SUCROSE_PPG","page":"Gravity and extract","text":"SUCROSE_PPG\n\nPoints per pound per gallon of sucrose, 46.214: one pound of sucrose in one US gallon of solution gives a specific gravity of 1.046214.\n\nReferences\n\nAbout 46 PPG is the value commonly given for sucrose in brewing references; the source of the precise value 46.214, carried over from usual brewing software, was not found. (The extract model of this package, with SugarGravityQuad, gives about 46.18.)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.SUCROSE_PPG"},{"category":"section","location":"mash/#Mash","page":"Mash","text":"Temperatures and volumes of the mash. The heat balances take the specific heat of the grain relative to that of water, grain_heat = 0.4 by default, and neglect the heat capacity of the vessel.","title":"Mash"},{"category":"function","location":"mash/#BrewingFormulas.strike_temperature","page":"Mash","text":"strike_temperature(target, grain_temperature, water, grain; grain_heat = 0.4)\n\nThe temperature of the strike water (a volume or mass) to reach the target mash temperature when mixed with a mass grain of grain at grain_temperature, by the heat balance T_w = T + (c_g m_g / m_w) (T - T_g).\n\nReferences\n\nJ. Palmer, How to Brew, chapter \"The Methods of Mashing\", initial infusion equation T_w = (0.2 / r)(T - T_g) + T, with the ratio r in qt/lb, https://howtobrew.com.\n\njulia> strike_temperature(67u\"°C\", 20u\"°C\", 15u\"L\", 5u\"kg\")\n73.2779481394733 °C\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.strike_temperature"},{"category":"function","location":"mash/#BrewingFormulas.infusion_volume","page":"Mash","text":"infusion_volume(current, target, water, grain; infusion_temperature = 100u\"°C\", grain_heat = 0.4)\n\nThe volume of water at infusion_temperature to add to a mash at temperature current, with the given water (volume or mass) and mass grain, to raise it to target: V = (T₂ - T₁)(c_g m_g + m_w) / (T_w - T₂).\n\nReferences\n\nJ. Palmer, How to Brew, chapter \"The Methods of Mashing\", mash infusion equation W_a = (T₂ - T₁)(0.2 G + W_m) / (T_w - T₂), https://howtobrew.com.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.infusion_volume"},{"category":"function","location":"mash/#BrewingFormulas.mash_thickness","page":"Mash","text":"mash_thickness(water, grain)\n\nThe ratio of the water volume to the grain mass, in L/kg.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.mash_thickness"},{"category":"function","location":"mash/#BrewingFormulas.mash_volume","page":"Mash","text":"mash_volume(water, grain; displacement = 0.67u\"L/kg\")\n\nThe total volume of a mash with the given water volume and grain mass, the grain displacing displacement per unit mass.\n\nReferences\n\nThe default, 0.32 qt/lb (0.67 L/kg), is from \"Calculating Water Usage\", Brew Your Own, https://byo.com/articles/calculating-water-usage/.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.mash_volume"},{"category":"function","location":"mash/#BrewingFormulas.grain_absorption","page":"Mash","text":"grain_absorption(grain; rate = 1.0u\"L/kg\")\n\nThe volume of liquid retained by a mass grain of spent grain, at the given absorption rate (usually from 0.8 to 1.25 L/kg).\n\nReferences\n\nAbout 0.1 gal/lb (0.83 L/kg) is the usual rule of thumb, e.g. in \"How To Brew in a Bag\", American Homebrewers Association, https://www.homebrewersassociation.org/wp-content/uploads/How-To-Brew-in-a-Bag.pdf; brewing software uses from about 0.7 to 1.2 L/kg. The default here, 1.0 L/kg, is in this range and should be adjusted to the equipment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.grain_absorption"},{"category":"function","location":"mash/#BrewingFormulas.moisture_water","page":"Mash","text":"moisture_water(grain, moisture)\n\nThe volume of water (at 20 °C) brought by a mass grain of fermentable with the given moisture content (a percentage or fraction).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.moisture_water"},{"category":"section","location":"#BrewingFormulas.jl","page":"Home","text":"Formulas used in beer brewing calculations, as plain stateless Julia functions working with Unitful.jl quantities and the brewing units of UnitfulBrewing.jl. It covers gravity and extract, attenuation and alcohol, refractometer corrections, color, bitterness, mash and boil volumes and temperatures, carbonation and yeast pitching.\n\nIt is the formula layer of the BrewQuiver brewing software, and can be used on its own as a brewing calculator at the Julia REPL.\n\nnote: Note\nThis package is still under development and not yet registered.","title":"BrewingFormulas.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"Since neither this package nor UnitfulBrewing is registered yet, install them from their repositories:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(url = \"https://github.com/JuliaBrewing/UnitfulBrewing.jl\")\nPkg.add(url = \"https://github.com/JuliaBrewing/BrewingFormulas.jl\")","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"julia> using Unitful, UnitfulBrewing, BrewingFormulas\n\njulia> abv(1.050u\"sg\", 1.010u\"sg\")\n5.316690074810317 %\n\njulia> abv(SimpleABV(), 1.050u\"sg\", 1.010u\"sg\")\n5.250000000000004 %\n\njulia> ibu(30u\"g\", 10u\"percent\", 1.050u\"sg\", 60u\"minute\", 20u\"L\")\n34.59961158766487 IBU\n\njulia> wort_color([4u\"kg\" => 3u\"°L\", 0.3u\"kg\" => 120u\"EBC\"], 20u\"L\")\n7.586923163856394 SRM\n\njulia> strike_temperature(67u\"°C\", 20u\"°C\", 15u\"L\", 5u\"kg\")\n73.2779481394733 °C\n\nThe arguments can be given in any unit of the right dimension, and the results are quantities, which can be converted to other units with uconvert.","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Contents","page":"Home","text":"Conventions and models: how arguments are given, and how alternative formulas are selected by dispatch.\nFormulas: gravity and extract, attenuation and alcohol, refractometer, color, bitterness, mash, boil and volumes, carbonation and yeast.\nReferences: the sources of all the formulas.","title":"Contents"},{"category":"section","location":"#License","page":"Home","text":"This package is licensed under the MIT license.","title":"License"},{"category":"section","location":"water/#Water","page":"Water","text":"The chemistry of brewing water: the ions added by salts, alkalinity and hardness, Kolbach's residual alkalinity, the protons given by acids, and an estimate of the mash pH by a balance of charges. Concentrations are in mg/L (or ppm); alkalinity, hardness and residual alkalinity are in mg/L as CaCO₃, one milliequivalent per liter being 50.04 mg/L as CaCO₃.","title":"Water"},{"category":"function","location":"water/#BrewingFormulas.alkalinity","page":"Water","text":"alkalinity(bicarbonate)\n\nThe alkalinity, in mg/L as CaCO₃, of water with the given concentration of bicarbonate (HCO₃⁻, in mg/L or ppm), by the equivalent weights of HCO₃⁻ and CaCO₃ (the other carbonate species are negligible at the pH of brewing water).\n\njulia> ustrip(u\"mg/L\", alkalinity(122u\"mg/L\"))   # mg/L as CaCO₃\n100.0597548184083\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.alkalinity"},{"category":"function","location":"water/#BrewingFormulas.calcium_hardness","page":"Water","text":"calcium_hardness(calcium)\n\nThe hardness, in mg/L as CaCO₃, of the given concentration of calcium (mg/L or ppm).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.calcium_hardness"},{"category":"function","location":"water/#BrewingFormulas.magnesium_hardness","page":"Water","text":"magnesium_hardness(magnesium)\n\nThe hardness, in mg/L as CaCO₃, of the given concentration of magnesium (mg/L or ppm).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.magnesium_hardness"},{"category":"function","location":"water/#BrewingFormulas.milliequivalents","page":"Water","text":"milliequivalents(x)\n\nThe milliequivalents per liter, as a plain number, of a concentration x in mg/L as CaCO₃ (alkalinity, hardness, residual alkalinity): one milliequivalent per liter is 50.04 mg/L as CaCO₃.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.milliequivalents"},{"category":"function","location":"water/#BrewingFormulas.residual_alkalinity","page":"Water","text":"residual_alkalinity(alkalinity, calcium, magnesium)\n\nKolbach's residual alkalinity, in mg/L as CaCO₃, of water with the given alkalinity (in mg/L as CaCO₃) and concentrations of calcium and magnesium (mg/L or ppm): the alkalinity left after the calcium and magnesium react with the phosphates of the malt, 3.5 equivalents of calcium and 7 of magnesium neutralizing one equivalent of alkalinity, RA = alkalinity - calcium hardness / 3.5 - magnesium hardness / 7.\n\nReferences\n\nP. Kolbach (1953), as given in J. Palmer and C. Kaminski, Water: A Comprehensive Guide for Brewers, Brewers Publications, 2013, in \"The Power of pH\", Brew Your Own (https://byo.com/?p=8960), and in MEBAK method W-030.01.900, \"Residual Alkalinity in Brewing Liquor\". The original paper was not found.\n\njulia> ustrip(u\"mg/L\", residual_alkalinity(100u\"mg/L\", 50u\"mg/L\", 10u\"mg/L\"))\n58.44182691724805\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.residual_alkalinity"},{"category":"type","location":"water/#BrewingFormulas.AbstractSalt","page":"Water","text":"AbstractSalt\n\nBrewing salts, characterized by the ions they add per mass dissolved in a volume of water (see ions): Gypsum, CalciumChloride, EpsomSalt, MagnesiumChloride, TableSalt, BakingSoda, Chalk, SlakedLime.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.AbstractSalt"},{"category":"type","location":"water/#BrewingFormulas.Gypsum","page":"Water","text":"Gypsum, calcium sulfate dihydrate, CaSO₄·2H₂O.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.Gypsum"},{"category":"type","location":"water/#BrewingFormulas.CalciumChloride","page":"Water","text":"Calcium chloride dihydrate, CaCl₂·2H₂O.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.CalciumChloride"},{"category":"type","location":"water/#BrewingFormulas.EpsomSalt","page":"Water","text":"Epsom salt, magnesium sulfate heptahydrate, MgSO₄·7H₂O.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.EpsomSalt"},{"category":"type","location":"water/#BrewingFormulas.MagnesiumChloride","page":"Water","text":"Magnesium chloride hexahydrate, MgCl₂·6H₂O.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.MagnesiumChloride"},{"category":"type","location":"water/#BrewingFormulas.TableSalt","page":"Water","text":"Table salt, sodium chloride, NaCl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.TableSalt"},{"category":"type","location":"water/#BrewingFormulas.BakingSoda","page":"Water","text":"Baking soda, sodium bicarbonate, NaHCO₃.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.BakingSoda"},{"category":"type","location":"water/#BrewingFormulas.Chalk","page":"Water","text":"Chalk, calcium carbonate, CaCO₃; it barely dissolves in water, but does in the mash.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.Chalk"},{"category":"type","location":"water/#BrewingFormulas.SlakedLime","page":"Water","text":"Slaked lime, calcium hydroxide, Ca(OH)₂.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.SlakedLime"},{"category":"function","location":"water/#BrewingFormulas.ions","page":"Water","text":"ions(salt, mass, volume)\n\nThe concentrations, as a NamedTuple (calcium, magnesium, sodium, chloride, sulfate, alkalinity) in mg/L (the alkalinity as CaCO₃), that a mass of the AbstractSalt salt adds to a volume of water, from the stoichiometry of the salt and the IUPAC standard atomic weights.\n\njulia> ustrip(u\"mg/L\", ions(Gypsum(), 1u\"g\", 1u\"L\").calcium)\n232.78966566761923\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.ions"},{"category":"type","location":"water/#BrewingFormulas.AbstractAcid","page":"Water","text":"AbstractAcid\n\nAcids for lowering the pH of mash or sparge water, as solutions of a given strength (mass fraction) and density: LacticAcid, PhosphoricAcid. See acid_milliequivalents.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.AbstractAcid"},{"category":"type","location":"water/#BrewingFormulas.LacticAcid","page":"Water","text":"LacticAcid(; strength = 88u\"percent\", density = 1.21u\"g/mL\")\n\nA solution of lactic acid (pKa 3.86), by default the usual 88% solution, whose density is about 1.21 g/mL.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.LacticAcid"},{"category":"type","location":"water/#BrewingFormulas.PhosphoricAcid","page":"Water","text":"PhosphoricAcid(; strength = 10u\"percent\", density = 1.05u\"g/mL\")\n\nA solution of phosphoric acid (pKa 2.15, 7.20, 12.35), by default a 10% solution, whose density is about 1.05 g/mL (85% solutions are about 1.69 g/mL).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.PhosphoricAcid"},{"category":"function","location":"water/#BrewingFormulas.acid_milliequivalents","page":"Water","text":"acid_milliequivalents(acid, volume; ph = 5.4)\n\nThe milliequivalents of protons, as a plain number, that a volume of the solution of AbstractAcid acid gives at the pH ph (of a mash, about 5.4), from its strength, density, molar mass and pKa values (the fraction of each acid group dissociated at ph).\n\nReferences\n\nThe pKa values are from the CRC Handbook of Chemistry and Physics; the densities of the solutions are typical values, to be checked against the supplier's.\n\njulia> acid_milliequivalents(LacticAcid(), 1u\"mL\")\n11.489505404836624\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.acid_milliequivalents"},{"category":"constant","location":"water/#BrewingFormulas.BASE_MALT_DI_PH","page":"Water","text":"BASE_MALT_DI_PH\n\nThe usual pH of a mash of pale base malt in distilled water, 5.7, the default of mash_ph: a rule of thumb, from secondary sources. The distilled water pH of crystal and roasted malts is much lower, and must be given.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.BASE_MALT_DI_PH"},{"category":"constant","location":"water/#BrewingFormulas.MALT_BUFFERING","page":"Water","text":"MALT_BUFFERING\n\nThe usual buffering capacity of malts, 40 mEq/(kg pH), the default of mash_ph: a rule of thumb, from secondary sources, consistent with the shift of about 0.1 pH per mEq/L of residual alkalinity at 4 L/kg reported by Troester.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.MALT_BUFFERING"},{"category":"function","location":"water/#BrewingFormulas.mash_ph","page":"Water","text":"mash_ph(grist, water, residual_alkalinity; acids = [])\n\nAn estimate of the pH of a mash, by a balance of charges: each malt of the grist resists moving from its distilled water pH with its buffering capacity, and the water and the acids push it,\n\nΣ mᵢ bᵢ (pH - pHᵢ) = V RA - acid,\n\nwith the masses mᵢ (kg), buffering capacities bᵢ (mEq/(kg pH)) and distilled water pH pHᵢ of the malts, the volume V of water (L), its Kolbach residual alkalinity RA (mEq/L, from mg/L as CaCO₃, see residual_alkalinity), and the milliequivalents of acids (pairs acid => volume, see acid_milliequivalents, at the resulting pH).\n\nEach malt is a NamedTuple (mass, di_ph, buffering); di_ph and buffering may be nothing, for the defaults BASE_MALT_DI_PH and MALT_BUFFERING, right only for pale base malts. The pH is returned as a plain number.\n\nReferences\n\nThe model of a balance of charges with linear buffering, in K. Troester, \"The Effect of Brewing Water and Grist Composition on the pH of the Mash\", braukaiser.com, 2009, and A. J. deLange's notes on estimating the mash pH, as described in J. Palmer, \"Exploring the effect of residual alkalinity\", ASBC 2016 (https://www.asbcnet.org/events/archives/2016/proceedings/Documents/GC_Water_Palmer.pdf), which reports about 0.1 pH per mEq/L of residual alkalinity at 4 L/kg, as given here by a buffering of 40 mEq/(kg pH). The original papers were not found.\n\njulia> mash_ph([(mass = 5u\"kg\", di_ph = nothing, buffering = nothing)], 15u\"L\", 50u\"mg/L\")\n5.774935555422337\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.mash_ph"},{"category":"section","location":"carbonation/#Carbonation","page":"Carbonation","text":"The CO₂ left in the beer after fermentation, the priming sugar to reach a given carbonation, and the pressure for forced carbonation.","title":"Carbonation"},{"category":"function","location":"carbonation/#BrewingFormulas.residual_co2","page":"Carbonation","text":"residual_co2(temperature)\n\nThe CO₂, in vols, remaining dissolved in a beer after fermentation at temperature (the highest temperature reached after fermentation), at 1 atm: 3.0378 - 0.050062 T + 0.00026555 T², with T in °F.\n\nReferences\n\nM. L. Hall, \"Brew By the Numbers — Add Up What's in Your Beer\", Zymurgy, Summer 1995, pp. 54–61, Equation 26.\n\njulia> residual_co2(20u\"°C\")\n0.8614871999999993 vols\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.residual_co2"},{"category":"function","location":"carbonation/#BrewingFormulas.priming_sugar","page":"Carbonation","text":"priming_sugar(sugar, volume, target, temperature)\n\nThe mass, in g, of priming sugar to bring a volume of beer, fermented up to temperature, to the target carbonation (in vols), using 1 vols = 1.977 g/L of CO₂.\n\nThe mass of sugar follows from the stoichiometry of its fermentation (see co2_yield). Hall's formula PS = 15.195 V(gal) (CD - CD₀), in grams of corn sugar, corresponds to anhydrous glucose; here CornSugar is dextrose monohydrate, as usually sold, which needs about 10% more. Use Glucose for Hall's values.\n\nReferences\n\nM. L. Hall, \"Brew By the Numbers — Add Up What's in Your Beer\", Zymurgy, Summer 1995, pp. 54–61, Equation 29.\n\njulia> priming_sugar(CornSugar(), 20u\"L\", 2.5u\"vols\", 20u\"°C\")\n145.86469026359262 g\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.priming_sugar"},{"category":"type","location":"carbonation/#BrewingFormulas.AbstractPrimingSugar","page":"Carbonation","text":"AbstractPrimingSugar\n\nSugars for priming, characterized by their co2_yield, the mass of CO₂ produced per mass of sugar fully fermented: Sucrose, Glucose, CornSugar, or a custom PrimingSugar(yield), e.g. for malt extracts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.AbstractPrimingSugar"},{"category":"type","location":"carbonation/#BrewingFormulas.PrimingSugar","page":"Carbonation","text":"PrimingSugar(yield)\n\nA priming sugar producing the mass fraction yield of CO₂ when fermented, e.g. for malt extracts, which are only partially fermentable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.PrimingSugar"},{"category":"type","location":"carbonation/#BrewingFormulas.Sucrose","page":"Carbonation","text":"Sucrose, C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁ + H₂O → 4 C₂H₅OH + 4 CO₂.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.Sucrose"},{"category":"type","location":"carbonation/#BrewingFormulas.Glucose","page":"Carbonation","text":"Anhydrous glucose (dextrose), C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2 C₂H₅OH + 2 CO₂.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.Glucose"},{"category":"type","location":"carbonation/#BrewingFormulas.CornSugar","page":"Carbonation","text":"Corn sugar, i.e. dextrose monohydrate, C₆H₁₂O₆·H₂O.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.CornSugar"},{"category":"function","location":"carbonation/#BrewingFormulas.co2_yield","page":"Carbonation","text":"co2_yield(sugar)\n\nMass of CO₂ produced per mass of the priming sugar, assuming full fermentation, from the stoichiometry of the fermentation and the molar masses of the sugar and of CO₂ (IUPAC standard atomic weights).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.co2_yield"},{"category":"function","location":"carbonation/#BrewingFormulas.carbonation_pressure","page":"Carbonation","text":"carbonation_pressure(level, temperature)\n\nThe gauge pressure, in psi, of CO₂ to force carbonate a beer at temperature to the given carbonation level (in vols), from the fit of the carbonation charts used by most brewing calculators (T in °F, V in vols, P in psig):\n\nP = -16.6999 - 0.0101059 T + 0.00116512 T² + 0.173354 T V + 4.24267 V - 0.0684226 V².\n\njulia> carbonation_pressure(2.5u\"vols\", 38u\"°F\")\n11.246172830000003 psi\n\nReferences\n\nNo primary source for this fit was found; it is the \"standard force carbonation equation\" of many brewing calculators, a fit to the published carbonation charts (CO₂ solubility tables such as those of the ASBC).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.carbonation_pressure"},{"category":"function","location":"carbonation/#BrewingFormulas.carbonation_level","page":"Carbonation","text":"carbonation_level(pressure, temperature)\n\nThe carbonation, in vols, reached at equilibrium under the gauge pressure of CO₂ at temperature; the inverse of carbonation_pressure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.carbonation_level"},{"category":"section","location":"attenuation/#Attenuation-and-alcohol","page":"Attenuation and alcohol","text":"The relations between the original, real and apparent extracts and the alcohol content, after Balling, and the energy content of beer.","title":"Attenuation and alcohol"},{"category":"function","location":"attenuation/#BrewingFormulas.apparent_attenuation","page":"Attenuation and alcohol","text":"apparent_attenuation(og, fg; eq = SugarGravityQuad())\n\nThe apparent attenuation 1 - AE / OE, from the original gravity og and the (apparent) final gravity fg, with the extracts in °P.\n\njulia> apparent_attenuation(15u\"°P\", 3.83u\"°P\")\n74.46666666666665 %\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.apparent_attenuation"},{"category":"function","location":"attenuation/#BrewingFormulas.real_attenuation","page":"Attenuation and alcohol","text":"real_attenuation(og, fg; eq = SugarGravityQuad())\n\nThe real attenuation 1 - RE / OE, from the original gravity og and the apparent final gravity fg.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.real_attenuation"},{"category":"function","location":"attenuation/#BrewingFormulas.real_extract","page":"Attenuation and alcohol","text":"real_extract(og, fg; eq = SugarGravityQuad())\n\nThe real extract RE = 0.1808 OE + 0.8192 AE (Balling), in °P, from the original gravity og and the apparent final gravity fg.\n\nReferences\n\nJ. De Clerck, A Textbook of Brewing, Chapman & Hall, 1958, after C. J. N. Balling.\nM. L. Hall, \"Brew By the Numbers — Add Up What's in Your Beer\", Zymurgy, Summer 1995, pp. 54–61 (Equation 11).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.real_extract"},{"category":"function","location":"attenuation/#BrewingFormulas.apparent_extract","page":"Attenuation and alcohol","text":"apparent_extract(og, re; eq = SugarGravityQuad())\n\nThe apparent extract, in the unit of og, from the original gravity og and the real extract re; the inverse of real_extract.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.apparent_extract"},{"category":"function","location":"attenuation/#BrewingFormulas.final_gravity","page":"Attenuation and alcohol","text":"final_gravity(og, attenuation; eq = SugarGravityQuad())\n\nThe apparent final gravity, in the unit of og, reached from the original gravity og with the given apparent attenuation (a percentage or fraction): AE = (1 - a) OE.\n\njulia> final_gravity(1.050u\"sg\", 75u\"percent\")\n1.0121194807789198 sg\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.final_gravity"},{"category":"function","location":"attenuation/#BrewingFormulas.abw","page":"Attenuation and alcohol","text":"abw(og, fg; eq = SugarGravityQuad())\n\nThe alcohol by weight, from the original gravity og and the apparent final gravity fg, by Balling's relation ABW = (OE - RE) / (2.0665 - 0.010665 OE).\n\nReferences\n\nJ. De Clerck, A Textbook of Brewing, Chapman & Hall, 1958, after C. J. N. Balling.\nM. L. Hall, \"Brew By the Numbers — Add Up What's in Your Beer\", Zymurgy, Summer 1995, pp. 54–61 (Equation 16).\n\njulia> abw(15u\"°P\", 3.83u\"°P\")\n4.799551015591193 %\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.abw"},{"category":"function","location":"attenuation/#BrewingFormulas.abv","page":"Attenuation and alcohol","text":"abv([model = Balling()], og, fg; eq = SugarGravityQuad())\n\nThe alcohol by volume, from the original gravity og and the apparent final gravity fg, with the given ABVModel:\n\nBalling: ABV = ABW × FG / ρ_ethanol;\nSimpleABV: ABV = 131.25 (OG - FG), gravities in sg.\n\nSee the models for the references.\n\njulia> abv(1.050u\"sg\", 1.010u\"sg\")\n5.316690074810317 %\n\njulia> abv(SimpleABV(), 1.050u\"sg\", 1.010u\"sg\")\n5.250000000000004 %\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.abv"},{"category":"function","location":"attenuation/#BrewingFormulas.real_extract_from_abw","page":"Attenuation and alcohol","text":"real_extract_from_abw(og, abw; eq = SugarGravityQuad())\n\nThe real extract, in °P, of a beer with original gravity og and alcohol by weight abw; the inverse of abw in terms of the real extract.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.real_extract_from_abw"},{"category":"function","location":"attenuation/#BrewingFormulas.calories","page":"Attenuation and alcohol","text":"calories(og, fg, volume; eq = SugarGravityQuad())\n\nThe energy, in kcal, of a volume of beer with original gravity og and apparent final gravity fg: FG × V(mL) × (6.9 ABW + 4.0 (RE - 0.1)) / 100, with ABW in percent by weight and RE in °P: 6.9 kcal per gram of alcohol and 4 kcal per gram of real extract less 0.1% of ash, per 100 g of beer, as in the Python BrewQuiver.\n\nReferences\n\nG. Oliver (ed.), The Oxford Companion to Beer, Oxford University Press, 2011, entry \"calories\", as quoted in \"Counting Calories & Carbs\", Brew Your Own, https://byo.com/articles/counting-calories-carbs/.\nM. L. Hall, \"Brew By the Numbers — Add Up What's in Your Beer\", Zymurgy, Summer 1995, pp. 54–61, Equation 22, gives a variant per 12 US fl oz, 3.55 FG (4.08 RE + 7.1 ABW), which also counts the protein.\n\njulia> calories(15u\"°P\", 3.83u\"°P\", 330u\"mL\")\n187.9616874164696 kcal\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.calories"},{"category":"constant","location":"attenuation/#BrewingFormulas.ETHANOL_RELATIVE_DENSITY","page":"Attenuation and alcohol","text":"ETHANOL_RELATIVE_DENSITY\n\nDensity of ethanol relative to water, both at 20 °C: 0.78924 / 0.998203 ≈ 0.7907, used to convert alcohol by weight into alcohol by volume. Hall (1995) uses 0.794, the specific gravity of ethanol at 15 °C, which gives ABVs about 0.4% (relative) lower.\n\nReferences\n\nDensity of ethanol at 20 °C, 0.78924 g/mL: CRC Handbook of Chemistry and Physics.\nG. S. Kell, \"Density, thermal expansivity, and compressibility of liquid water from 0° to 150 °C\", J. Chem. Eng. Data 20 (1975) 97–105, https://doi.org/10.1021/je60064a005.\nM. L. Hall, \"Brew By the Numbers — Add Up What's in Your Beer\", Zymurgy, Summer 1995, pp. 54–61.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.ETHANOL_RELATIVE_DENSITY"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#Conventions-and-models","page":"Conventions and models","text":"","title":"Conventions and models"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#Arguments","page":"Conventions and models","text":"Units: arguments are Unitful quantities in any unit of the right dimension, and results are quantities.\nGravities are accepted in °P, Brix, sg or gu, and must carry their unit. A plain number is rejected, since 1.050 and 12 are both plausible gravities, and so is a percentage. Results derived from a gravity come back in the same unit family:\njulia> final_gravity(1.050u\"sg\", 75u\"percent\")\n1.0121194807789198 sg\n\njulia> final_gravity(12u\"°P\", 75u\"percent\")\n3.0 °P\n\njulia> plato(1.050)\nERROR: ArgumentError: a gravity must carry its unit (°P, Brix, sg or gu), got 1.05; e.g. 1.050u\"sg\" or 12u\"°P\"\n[...]\nConversions between sugar contents and specific gravity use the equivalence given by the keyword eq, by default SugarGravityQuad() from UnitfulBrewing.\nFractions (attenuation, alpha acids, moisture, ...) are percent quantities, or plain numbers taken as fractions: 75u\"percent\" and 0.75 are the same.","title":"Arguments"},{"category":"section","location":"conventions/#Models","page":"Conventions and models","text":"When several empirical formulas exist for the same quantity, each one is a model: a singleton type passed as the first argument and selected by dispatch. Without a model, the default one in DEFAULT_MODELS is used:\n\njulia> srm(10)\n7.239807780133753 SRM\n\njulia> srm(Daniels(), 10)\n10.4 SRM\n\nQuantity Models Default\nsugar contents ↔ gravity SugarGravityQuad, SugarGravity (keyword eq) SugarGravityQuad()\nhop utilization, IBU Tinseth, Rager Tinseth()\nMCU → SRM Morey, Daniels, Mosher, DrueyDaniels, DrueyNoonan Morey()\nalcohol by volume Balling, SimpleABV Balling()\nrefractometer final gravity TerrillCubic, TerrillLinear TerrillCubic()\n\nNew models are added by defining a subtype of the corresponding abstract type and a method for it, without changing this package.","title":"Models"},{"category":"module","location":"conventions/#BrewingFormulas","page":"Conventions and models","text":"BrewingFormulas\n\nFormulas used in beer brewing calculations: gravity and extract, attenuation and alcohol, refractometer corrections, color, bitterness, mash and boil volumes and temperatures, carbonation and yeast pitching.\n\nAll functions are stateless and work with Unitful.jl quantities, with the brewing units of UnitfulBrewing.jl. Arguments may be given in any unit of the right dimension, and results are quantities.\n\nWhen several empirical formulas exist for the same thing, each one is a model, a singleton type passed as the first argument, e.g. utilization(Tinseth(), og, 60u\"minute\") or srm(Morey(), mcu). Calling the function without a model uses the default one, listed in DEFAULT_MODELS.\n\nConventions for the arguments\n\nGravities and extracts are accepted in °P, Brix, sg or gu, and must carry their unit: a plain number is rejected, since 1.050 and 12 are both plausible gravities. Conversions between sugar contents and specific gravity use the equivalence given by the keyword eq, SugarGravityQuad() by default.\nFractions (attenuation, alpha acids, moisture, ...) are accepted as percent quantities or as plain numbers, which are taken as fractions: 75percent and 0.75 are the same.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas"},{"category":"constant","location":"conventions/#BrewingFormulas.DEFAULT_EQUIVALENCE","page":"Conventions and models","text":"DEFAULT_EQUIVALENCE\n\nThe equivalence between sugar contents (°P) and specific gravity (sg) used by default, SugarGravityQuad(), from UnitfulBrewing: °P = 668.72 sg - 463.37 - 205.35 sg².\n\nReferences\n\nThis quadratic fit is attributed to Robbins (1975) in \"On the relationship between Plato and specific gravity\", Brewer's Friend, 2012, https://www.brewersfriend.com/2012/10/31/on-the-relationship-between-plato-and-specific-gravity; the original publication was not found.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.DEFAULT_EQUIVALENCE"},{"category":"constant","location":"conventions/#BrewingFormulas.WATER_DENSITY_20C","page":"Conventions and models","text":"WATER_DENSITY_20C\n\nDensity of water at 20 °C, the reference temperature of the Plato tables (sg 20/20).\n\nReferences\n\nG. S. Kell, \"Density, thermal expansivity, and compressibility of liquid water from 0° to 150 °C\", J. Chem. Eng. Data 20 (1975) 97–105, https://doi.org/10.1021/je60064a005.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.WATER_DENSITY_20C"},{"category":"constant","location":"conventions/#BrewingFormulas.DEFAULT_MODELS","page":"Conventions and models","text":"DEFAULT_MODELS\n\nThe models used when none is given:\n\ngravity = SugarGravityQuad(), the equivalence between °P and sg;\nbitterness = Tinseth();\ncolor = Morey();\nabv = Balling();\nrefractometer = TerrillCubic().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.DEFAULT_MODELS"},{"category":"type","location":"conventions/#BrewingFormulas.AbstractModel","page":"Conventions and models","text":"AbstractModel\n\nSupertype of the models, the singleton types that select one of several empirical formulas for the same quantity, by dispatch. New models are added by defining a subtype and the corresponding methods, e.g.\n\nstruct MyUtilization <: BitternessModel end\nBrewingFormulas.utilization(::MyUtilization, gravity, time; eq = SugarGravityQuad()) = ...\n\nThe sources of the formulas are given in each docstring and collected in the file REFERENCES.md of the package.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.AbstractModel"},{"category":"type","location":"conventions/#BrewingFormulas.BitternessModel","page":"Conventions and models","text":"BitternessModel\n\nModels for the utilization of alpha acids in the boil: Tinseth, Rager.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.BitternessModel"},{"category":"type","location":"conventions/#BrewingFormulas.Tinseth","page":"Conventions and models","text":"Tinseth()\n\nGlenn Tinseth's model for the utilization of alpha acids, utilization = 1.65 × 0.000125^(sg - 1) × (1 - exp(-0.04 t)) / 4.15, with t in minutes: the product of a \"bigness factor\", accounting for the gravity of the wort, and a \"boil time factor\".\n\nTinseth's model was not derived for pellets; the usual increase of about 10% for pellets is a homebrewing convention, applied here through the keyword factor.\n\nReferences\n\nG. Tinseth, hop utilization page, http://www.realbeer.com/hops/research.html (the realbeer.com site has since been retired); also in N. Pyle's Hops FAQ, http://www.realbeer.com/hops/FAQ.html, the source cited by the Python BrewQuiver.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.Tinseth"},{"category":"type","location":"conventions/#BrewingFormulas.Rager","page":"Conventions and models","text":"Rager()\n\nJackie Rager's model for the utilization of alpha acids, utilization = (18.11 + 13.86 tanh((t - 31.32) / 18.27)) / 100 / (1 + GA), with t in minutes and the gravity adjustment GA = max(0, (sg - 1.050) / 0.2), sg being the gravity of the boiling wort.\n\nRager published the utilization as a table of boil times, together with the gravity adjustment; the tanh expression is a curve fit to that table, as given in Norm Pyle's Hops FAQ.\n\nReferences\n\nJ. Rager, \"Calculating Hop Bitterness in Beer\", Zymurgy, Special Issue 1990, pp. 53–54 (reproduced at https://bwelman.github.io/bier/calculating-hop-bitterness/hop-bitterness.html).\nN. Pyle, Hops FAQ, http://www.realbeer.com/hops/FAQ.html (the source cited by the Python BrewQuiver).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.Rager"},{"category":"type","location":"conventions/#BrewingFormulas.ColorModel","page":"Conventions and models","text":"ColorModel\n\nModels converting malt color units (MCU) to SRM: Morey, Daniels, Mosher, DrueyDaniels, DrueyNoonan.\n\nReferences\n\nD. Morey, \"Approximating SRM Beer Color of Homebrew Based on Recipe Formulation\", 2015, https://bwelman.github.io/bier/beer-color/beer-color.html, which compares all of them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.ColorModel"},{"category":"type","location":"conventions/#BrewingFormulas.Morey","page":"Conventions and models","text":"Morey()\n\nDan Morey's power model, SRM = 1.4922 MCU^0.6859, recommended for colors up to 50 SRM.\n\nReferences\n\nD. Morey, \"Approximating SRM Beer Color of Homebrew Based on Recipe Formulation\", 2015, https://bwelman.github.io/bier/beer-color/beer-color.html.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.Morey"},{"category":"type","location":"conventions/#BrewingFormulas.Daniels","page":"Conventions and models","text":"Daniels()\n\nRay Daniels' linear model, SRM = 0.2 MCU + 8.4, from homebrew competition samples; meant for darker beers, as it gives at least 8.4 SRM.\n\nReferences\n\nR. Daniels, Designing Great Beers: The Ultimate Guide to Brewing Classic Beer Styles, Brewers Publications, 1998.\nD. Morey (2015), see Morey, for the formula as quoted here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.Daniels"},{"category":"type","location":"conventions/#BrewingFormulas.Mosher","page":"Conventions and models","text":"Mosher()\n\nRandy Mosher's linear model, SRM = 0.3 MCU + 4.7, from commercial beers of known recipe and color; meant for darker beers, as it gives at least 4.7 SRM.\n\nReferences\n\nD. Morey (2015), see Morey, for the formula and its attribution to R. Mosher.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.Mosher"},{"category":"type","location":"conventions/#BrewingFormulas.DrueyDaniels","page":"Conventions and models","text":"DrueyDaniels()\n\nKyle Druey's power fit to Ray Daniels' data, SRM = 1.73 MCU^0.64 - 0.27 (named \"Druey-Daniels\" in the Python BrewQuiver).\n\nReferences\n\nK. Druey, Home Brew Digest, as reported by D. Morey (2015), see Morey.\nR. Daniels, Designing Great Beers, Brewers Publications, 1998, p. 61 (the data).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.DrueyDaniels"},{"category":"type","location":"conventions/#BrewingFormulas.DrueyNoonan","page":"Conventions and models","text":"DrueyNoonan()\n\nKyle Druey's power fit to Greg Noonan's data, SRM = 15.03 MCU^0.27 - 15.53 (named \"Druey-Noonan\" in the Python BrewQuiver).\n\nReferences\n\nK. Druey, Home Brew Digest, as reported by D. Morey (2015), see Morey.\nG. J. Noonan, New Brewing Lager Beer, revised and expanded edition, Brewers Publications, 2003, p. 206 (the data).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.DrueyNoonan"},{"category":"type","location":"conventions/#BrewingFormulas.ABVModel","page":"Conventions and models","text":"ABVModel\n\nModels for the alcohol by volume: Balling, SimpleABV.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.ABVModel"},{"category":"type","location":"conventions/#BrewingFormulas.Balling","page":"Conventions and models","text":"Balling()\n\nBalling's relations between original extract, real extract and alcohol: the real extract RE = 0.1808 OE + 0.8192 AE, the alcohol by weight ABW = (OE - RE) / (2.0665 - 0.010665 OE) and the alcohol by volume ABV = ABW × FG / ρ_ethanol, all extracts in °P.\n\nThe constant 2.0665 comes from Balling's mass balance of fermentation: 2.0665 g of extract give 1 g of ethanol, 0.9565 g of CO₂ and 0.11 g of yeast dry matter. For the relative density of ethanol, Hall uses 0.794; here it is ETHANOL_RELATIVE_DENSITY ≈ 0.7907, the ratio of the densities of ethanol and water at 20 °C, consistent with the Plato tables.\n\nReferences\n\nC. J. N. Balling, Die Gährungschemie, Prague, 1845–1865 (three editions).\nJ. De Clerck, A Textbook of Brewing, Chapman & Hall, 1958.\nM. L. Hall, \"Brew By the Numbers — Add Up What's in Your Beer\", Zymurgy, Summer 1995, pp. 54–61 (the relations as used here, after De Clerck).\nJ. Šavel, P. Košin, A. Brož, J. Vlček, \"Interpolation formulas for Balling's alcohol factors\", Kvasny prumysl 66 (2020) 239–244, https://doi.org/10.18832/kp2019.66.239 (the mass balance).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.Balling"},{"category":"type","location":"conventions/#BrewingFormulas.SimpleABV","page":"Conventions and models","text":"SimpleABV()\n\nThe usual homebrewing approximation ABV = 131.25 (OG - FG), with gravities in sg, adequate for usual beers but increasingly inaccurate for high gravities.\n\nReferences\n\nA widespread homebrewing formula; no primary source for it was found.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.SimpleABV"},{"category":"type","location":"conventions/#BrewingFormulas.RefractometerModel","page":"Conventions and models","text":"RefractometerModel\n\nModels for estimating the final gravity from refractometer readings: TerrillLinear, TerrillCubic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.RefractometerModel"},{"category":"type","location":"conventions/#BrewingFormulas.TerrillLinear","page":"Conventions and models","text":"TerrillLinear()\n\nSean Terrill's linear fit of the final gravity from the refractometer readings RIi and RIf (in Brix, divided by the wort correction factor) before and after fermentation: FG = 1 - 0.00085683 RIi + 0.0034941 RIf.\n\nReferences\n\nS. Terrill, \"Refractometer FG Results\", 7 April 2011, http://seanterrill.com/2011/04/07/refractometer-fg-results/ (cited by the Python BrewQuiver).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.TerrillLinear"},{"category":"type","location":"conventions/#BrewingFormulas.TerrillCubic","page":"Conventions and models","text":"TerrillCubic()\n\nSean Terrill's cubic fit of the final gravity from the refractometer readings RIi and RIf (in Brix, divided by the wort correction factor) before and after fermentation:\n\nFG = 1 - 0.0044993 RIi + 0.011774 RIf + 0.00027581 RIi² - 0.0012717 RIf²\n       - 0.0000072800 RIi³ + 0.000063293 RIf³.\n\nReferences\n\nS. Terrill, \"Refractometer FG Results\", 7 April 2011, http://seanterrill.com/2011/04/07/refractometer-fg-results/ (cited by the Python BrewQuiver).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BrewingFormulas.TerrillCubic"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#References","page":"References","text":"The sources of the formulas of BrewingFormulas, also given in the docstrings.\n\nThe column Python tells whether the Python BrewQuiver (2018), from which most formulas were ported, credited the formula. The column Checked tells how the attribution was verified for this package:\n\noriginal: in the original publication;\nsecondary: in a reliable secondary source quoting the original, which was not accessible;\nnot found: no primary source was found; the formula is credited as commonly used.\n\nFormula Function / model Source Python Checked\n°P ↔ sg, quadratic SugarGravityQuad (UnitfulBrewing) Robbins (1975), per [BF2012] no secondary\nDensity of water water_density, WATER_DENSITY_20C [Kell1975] — original (citation)\nExtract mass, solution volume extract_mass, solution_volume, ... definitions of °P and sg — —\nHydrometer correction hydrometer_correction [Lyons1992] no original\nSucrose 46.214 PPG SUCROSE_PPG ≈ 46 PPG is the usual value; 46.214 not found — not found\nReal extract, RE = 0.1808 OE + 0.8192 AE real_extract, Balling Balling, via [DeClerck1958], [Hall1995] Eq. 11 no original (Hall)\nAlcohol by weight abw, Balling Balling, via [DeClerck1958], [Hall1995] Eq. 16 no original (Hall)\nBalling's mass balance (2.0665 g → 1 + 0.9565 + 0.11 g) Balling [Savel2020] no original\nABV from ABW abv(::Balling, ...) [Hall1995] Eq. 18, with ρ = 0.794; here 0.7907 (20/20) no original\nABV = 131.25 (OG - FG) SimpleABV widespread homebrewing formula — not found\nCalories calories [Oliver2011], as quoted in [BYOcal]; variant in [Hall1995] Eq. 22 no secondary\nRefractometer FG, linear and cubic TerrillLinear, TerrillCubic [Terrill2011] yes secondary\nWort correction factor 1.04 refractometer_gravity common default (factor \"mosto\" in Python) no not found\nMCU mcu [Morey2015] no original\nMorey Morey [Morey2015] name only original\nDaniels Daniels [Daniels1998], as quoted in [Morey2015] no secondary\nMosher Mosher R. Mosher, as quoted in [Morey2015] no secondary\nDruey's fit to Daniels' data DrueyDaniels K. Druey (Home Brew Digest), in [Morey2015]; data [Daniels1998] p. 61 name only (\"Druey-Daniels\") secondary\nDruey's fit to Noonan's data DrueyNoonan K. Druey (Home Brew Digest), in [Morey2015]; data [Noonan2003] p. 206 name only (\"Druey-Noonan\") secondary\nSRM → RGB table srm_rgb, srm_hex unknown no not found\nTinseth utilization Tinseth [Tinseth], [Pyle] yes ([Pyle]) secondary\nRager utilization (tanh fit) Rager table and gravity adjustment [Rager1990]; tanh fit in [Pyle] yes ([Pyle]) secondary\nIsomerization rate (Arrhenius), relative rate below boiling isomerization_rate, isomerization_factor [Malowicki2005], constants as quoted by brewing calculators; they reproduce the relative rates quoted in [MTFhops] at 96 °C (72%) and 90 °C (43%), not its 17% at 82 °C (here 20%) no not found (constants)\nPellet factor +10% factor keyword (BrewQuiver: HOP_FORM_FACTORS) homebrewing convention, not part of Tinseth's model no not found\nIBU = 1 mg/L of iso-alpha acids ibu usual approximation — —\nStrike temperature, infusion strike_temperature, infusion_volume [Palmer] no secondary\nGrain displacement 0.67 L/kg mash_volume [BYOwater] no secondary\nGrain absorption grain_absorption rule of thumb ≈ 0.83 L/kg [AHAbiab]; software 0.7–1.2 L/kg no secondary\nResidual CO₂ residual_co2 [Hall1995] Eq. 26 no original\nPriming sugar priming_sugar, co2_yield stoichiometry; compare [Hall1995] Eq. 29 (anhydrous glucose) no original (Hall)\nForce carbonation pressure carbonation_pressure, carbonation_level fit to carbonation charts used by most calculators no not found\nIons of salts, alkalinity, hardness, mEq ions, alkalinity, calcium_hardness, magnesium_hardness, milliequivalents stoichiometry and equivalent weights, IUPAC standard atomic weights no —\nResidual alkalinity residual_alkalinity Kolbach (1953), as given in [PalmerKaminski2013], [BYOpH] and [MEBAKRA]; the original paper was not found no secondary\nAcids: dissociation acid_milliequivalents pKa of lactic (3.86) and phosphoric acid (2.15, 7.20, 12.35), [CRC]; the densities of the solutions are typical values no secondary\nMash pH, balance of charges mash_ph model of Troester (2009) and deLange, as described in [Palmer2016]; the defaults of 5.7 (pale malt in distilled water) and 40 mEq/(kg pH) are rules of thumb; the original papers were not found no secondary\nPitch rates 0.75 / 1.5 pitch_cells G. Fix, as in [White2010] no secondary\nThermal contraction, evaporation volume_at_temperature, evaporated_volume [Kell1975]; definitions — —","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#Bibliography","page":"References","text":"[AHAbiab] American Homebrewers Association, \"How To Brew in a Bag\", https://www.homebrewersassociation.org/wp-content/uploads/How-To-Brew-in-a-Bag.pdf.\n[BF2012] \"On the relationship between Plato and specific gravity\", Brewer's Friend, 31 October 2012, https://www.brewersfriend.com/2012/10/31/on-the-relationship-between-plato-and-specific-gravity.\n[BYOcal] \"Counting Calories & Carbs\", Brew Your Own, https://byo.com/articles/counting-calories-carbs/.\n[BYOwater] \"Calculating Water Usage\", Brew Your Own, https://byo.com/articles/calculating-water-usage/.\n[Daniels1998] R. Daniels, Designing Great Beers: The Ultimate Guide to Brewing Classic Beer Styles, Brewers Publications, 1998.\n[DeClerck1958] J. De Clerck, A Textbook of Brewing, Chapman & Hall, London, 1958. It presents C. J. N. Balling's relations, which Balling published between 1843 and 1865.\n[Hall1995] M. L. Hall, \"Brew By the Numbers — Add Up What's in Your Beer\", Zymurgy, Summer 1995, pp. 54–61.\n[Kell1975] G. S. Kell, \"Density, thermal expansivity, and compressibility of liquid water from 0° to 150 °C\", J. Chem. Eng. Data 20 (1975) 97–105, https://doi.org/10.1021/je60064a005.\n[Lyons1992] C. Lyons, \"Hydrometer Temperature Corrections\", Homebrew Digest #963, 7 September 1992, https://brewery.org/library/HydromCorr0992.html.\n[BYOpH] \"The Power of pH\", Brew Your Own, https://byo.com/?p=8960.\n[CRC] CRC Handbook of Chemistry and Physics, dissociation constants of organic and inorganic acids in aqueous solution.\n[MEBAKRA] MEBAK, method W-030.01.900, \"Residual Alkalinity in Brewing Liquor\", https://www.mebak.org/en/methode/w-030-01-900/residual-alkalinity-in-brewing-liquor/2988.\n[Malowicki2005] M. G. Malowicki and T. H. Shellhammer, \"Isomerization and Degradation Kinetics of Hop (Humulus lupulus) Acids in a Model Wort-Boiling System\", J. Agric. Food Chem. 53 (2005) 4434–4439, https://doi.org/10.1021/jf0481296.\n[MTFhops] \"Hops\", Milk The Funk Wiki, section on isomerization below boiling, https://www.milkthefunk.com/wiki/Hops.\n[Morey2015] D. Morey, \"Approximating SRM Beer Color of Homebrew Based on Recipe Formulation\", 2015, https://bwelman.github.io/bier/beer-color/beer-color.html.\n[Noonan2003] G. J. Noonan, New Brewing Lager Beer, revised and expanded edition, Brewers Publications, 2003.\n[Oliver2011] G. Oliver (ed.), The Oxford Companion to Beer, Oxford University Press,\n\n[Palmer2016] J. Palmer, \"And Plan B: Exploring the effect of residual alkalinity\", ASBC Annual Meeting 2016, https://www.asbcnet.org/events/archives/2016/proceedings/Documents/GC_Water_Palmer.pdf, presenting the work of K. Troester (\"The Effect of Brewing Water and Grist Composition on the pH of the Mash\", braukaiser.com, 2009) and A. J. deLange.\n[PalmerKaminski2013] J. Palmer and C. Kaminski, Water: A Comprehensive Guide for Brewers, Brewers Publications, 2013.\n[Palmer] J. Palmer, How to Brew, chapter \"The Methods of Mashing\", https://howtobrew.com.\n[Pyle] N. Pyle, Hops FAQ, http://www.realbeer.com/hops/FAQ.html (the realbeer.com site has been retired; the reference cited by the Python BrewQuiver).\n[Rager1990] J. Rager, \"Calculating Hop Bitterness in Beer\", Zymurgy, Special Issue 1990, pp. 53–54; reproduced at https://bwelman.github.io/bier/calculating-hop-bitterness/hop-bitterness.html.\n[Savel2020] J. Šavel, P. Košin, A. Brož, J. Vlček, \"Interpolation formulas for Balling's alcohol factors\", Kvasny prumysl 66 (2020) 239–244, https://doi.org/10.18832/kp2019.66.239.\n[Terrill2011] S. Terrill, \"Refractometer FG Results\", 7 April 2011, http://seanterrill.com/2011/04/07/refractometer-fg-results/ (the reference cited by the Python BrewQuiver).\n[Tinseth] G. Tinseth, hop utilization page, http://www.realbeer.com/hops/research.html (retired site).\n[White2010] C. White, J. Zainasheff, Yeast: The Practical Guide to Beer Fermentation, Brewers Publications, 2010.","title":"Bibliography"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#Notes-on-the-Python-BrewQuiver","page":"References","text":"Its abv_via_extrato_final_aparente passed the apparent final extract to conteudo_alcoolico_peso, which expects the real one; abv here uses the real extract, as in Balling's relation.\nIts options \"Druey-Daniels\" and \"Druey-Noonan\" are Kyle Druey's power fits, and are DrueyDaniels and DrueyNoonan here.\nIts Tinseth utilization was divided by 0.9 (\"since Tinseth is for leaf\"), with a factor 0.9 for leaf hops; here Tinseth's model is kept as published, and hop forms are handled by the factor keyword.","title":"Notes on the Python BrewQuiver"}]
}
